# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 1, ATTEMPT 4 on bagisto. THIS IS A RE-QUESTION, routed back from THREE measured
# two-arm mini-benches. The anchor is UNCHANGED (Webkul\Product\Models\Product); only the
# question and the gold group move, per ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR.
#
# TRAJECTORY - EVERY previous cycle, and which side of the window each fell on.
# The window is: plain arm <= 0.50 AND ours >= plain + 0.50. Both at once.
#   $ cat verticals/php-laravel/results/loop/bagisto/cycles.jsonl
#   {"cycle": 1, "attempt": 1, "baseline_dependents": 0.6471, "sense_dependents": 0.7647,
#    "title": "... audit what holds a product it never names"}
#   {"cycle": 2, "attempt": 2, "baseline_dependents": 0.8235, "sense_dependents": 0.8235,
#    "title": "... audit the kind-specific slices of the catalogue record"}
#   {"cycle": 3, "attempt": 3, "baseline_dependents": 0.9444, "sense_dependents": 0.7222,
#    "title": "... audit how the catalogue record arrives where it is used"}
#   (results/dryrun/bagisto/ holds minibench.1.md and minibench.2.md; results/loop/bagisto/
#    holds minibench.md and the three-line cycles.jsonl above. All three were read this
#    session, in full, not only the latest.)
#
#   cycle 1  baseline 0.6471  sense 0.7647  delta +0.1176
#            side: BASELINE-TOO-HIGH. Ceiling on any delta was 1.00-0.6471 = +0.3529.
#            Route: `grep -rn "\->product\b"`, widened four times, then confirmation reads.
#   cycle 2  baseline 0.8235  sense 0.8235  delta +0.0000
#            side: BASELINE-TOO-HIGH, further out. Ceiling +0.1765.
#            Route: one `grep -rn` over a seven-term disjunction of the per-kind collection
#            names, which the ASK had handed over. Second defect: the sense arm made 2 MCP
#            calls (sense_search, sense_status), no blast, no graph, and ran the plain route.
#   cycle 3  baseline 0.9444  sense 0.7222  delta -0.2222
#            side: BASELINE-TOO-HIGH, furthest out yet, and the instrument arm went BACKWARDS.
#            Ceiling +0.0556. `read_count: 0` on the plain arm in report.json - it never
#            opened a file; the accessor ladder (`function product(`, `->product`,
#            `$this->product`, `$x->product->`, `getTypeInstance()`) printed path:line for
#            17 of 18 rows for free.
#
# THE MONOTONE PATTERN, STATED AS A HYPOTHESIS AND NOT AS A FINDING.
# 0.6471 -> 0.8235 -> 0.9444 across three cycles, and the ask got LONGER and more explicit
# about how dependents hide at every step (cycle 3's step-2 prompt enumerates six route
# flavours in prose). Hypothesis: the elaboration is itself the leak - each clause describing
# a hiding route tells the plain arm what to search for. n=3, one run per arm per cycle, three
# different gold sets, so this is a hypothesis under test and not an established mechanism.
# The killers that would kill it and were RUN:
#   confound (does it co-vary with the gold set rather than the ask?) - the three gold sets
#     overlap in only 7 ids, so gold and ask move together and one CANNOT be separated from
#     the other on three points. NOT SEPARATED. Recorded as a live confound.
#   per-unit (does the aggregate hold per row?) - it does not, and this is the important one:
#     no dependents row has been missed by the plain arm in two consecutive cycles except
#     d:booking-price-note (missed in cycle 1 and cycle 3, taken in cycle 2). Quoted, from
#     scored.json missed_cite per cycle:
#       cycle 1 baseline missed: booking-price-note, admin-product-resource, admin-product-form,
#                                admin-shipment-inventory, admin-grouped-options,
#                                importer-type-family
#       cycle 2 baseline missed: repo-media-filetype, type-bundle-children,
#                                type-configurable-children
#       cycle 3 baseline missed: booking-price-note   (plus 2 non-scoring contract rows)
#     Every cycle-1 miss was TAKEN in cycles 2 and 3; every cycle-2 miss was TAKEN in cycle 3.
#     So "re-gold onto what it missed consistently" has almost no material to work with on
#     this anchor, and no row below is kept or cut on that ranking alone.
#   arithmetic - all three ceilings computed above; all three cells were dead before they ran.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL RISK THIS DRAFT CARRIES, NAMED UP FRONT.
# `bench/lib/gold.py` credits `cited` when the gold FILE path appears anywhere in the answer
# with any line pin attached (see its module docstring and `_cited`); the pinned line below is
# NOT compared against the agent's line. So citation cost on this repo is the cost of naming
# the FILE, and `grep -rn` prints a file with a line for free. That is the shape of all three
# failures. It is recorded here, it is not a gate, and it may not kill this draft: NO GREP
# SCREEN IS A GATE, and THE FIRST MEASUREMENT IS A REAL BASELINE ARM. The mini-bench decides.
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE.
# Correcting from BASELINE-TOO-HIGH for the fourth time. Two moves, in opposite directions
# from the last three cycles rather than further along the same line:
#   (a) THE ASK GETS SHORTER, NOT LONGER. Step 2 below is the shortest of the four attempts.
#       It names ONE mechanism in one clause and enumerates no route flavours, no relation
#       names, no collection names, no branch values and no subsystem categories. Cycles 1-3
#       each added description and each raised the plain arm; this moves the other way.
#   (b) THE QUESTION IS A CLASSIFICATION, NOT A CENSUS. Cycles 1-3 all asked WHICH PLACES
#       (hold / own a slice / receive) - three phrasings of "enumerate the dependents", which
#       is the occurrence kind and which a sweep prints. This asks, of each site, WHICH OF
#       SEVERAL INDEPENDENT RESOLUTIONS of the same stored value it is talking to. A site's
#       file and line do not answer that; the identity of the object it reached does.
# Intended landing: plain arm 0.25-0.45, ours 0.80+. The guard against over-shooting to the
# far side (the "neither arm gets anything" failure) is that all 18 rows sit inside a
# `direct_callers` row of the SHOWN blast payload with the enclosing routine spelled out, and
# the instrument arm has cited 13-14 of 17 in every cycle so far.
#
# THE AXIS - THE MECHANISM, IN ONE SENTENCE
# A record stores its kind as a plain string and, at runtime, looks a class up out of a config
# map keyed by that string, instantiates it and pushes ITSELF into it (Product.php:353 then
# :359) - so every question about what a record can do is answered by an object the record
# built, not by the record. The mechanism that hides the dependents is that THIS SAME
# RESOLUTION EXISTS IN MORE THAN ONE PLACE, INDEPENDENTLY: a cart line does it from its own
# copy of the string (CartItem.php:50), an order line does it from its own copy
# (OrderItem.php:59), a static helper does it from a bare string with no record involved at all
# (ProductType.php:18), and a handful of sites skip the object entirely and branch on the raw
# stored string themselves. A caller writing `$item->getTypeInstance()` and a caller writing
# `$product->getTypeInstance()` are two lines that look identical and reach two different
# objects resolved from two different copies of the value.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS:
# it is, per site, WHICH resolution the site is actually talking to - the record's own, a cart
# line's own, an order line's own, the static helper's, or none at all because the site
# branches on the raw string - and, where the site reaches one through a forwarder, the
# forwarding method that carries it. That is a classification of each site plus the identity
# of the object behind it. An occurrence list prints lines that mention a name; two of the
# lines below are byte-similar and belong to DIFFERENT classes (Cart.php:1058
# `$item->getTypeInstance()->isStockable()` resolves through the cart line's own copy;
# Models/Cart.php:137 `$item->product->isStockable()` resolves through the record's), and one
# line (OrderRepository.php:220 `if (! $item->isStockable())`) mentions neither the record nor
# the resolution and is a third class again.
#
# THE KIND (DECIDE, "the kind of question")
# Not the occurrence kind. It is closest to the possession kind - which object holds the
# behaviour a site depends on - and step 0b's ring is NON-EMPTY, measured this session:
#   $ python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#   Order    class  31 rows  31 files  15 vias   CANDIDATE -> scenario material (Loop 3)
#   Product  class  31 rows  31 files  15 vias   CANDIDATE -> scenario material (Loop 3)
# RECORDED, NOT USED: the ring is NOT served to the arm. The shown payload carries
# `retained_via_interfaces_count: 31` with `retained_trimmed: true` and ZERO rows, so no gold
# row below is taken from the ring. Every row comes from `direct_callers`, which IS shown.
#
# SEAM PROFILE - three candidates profiled (step 2), lowest precision first.
# PRECISION = structural dependent files / grep-hit files; LOWER IS BETTER.
#   $ python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Order --propose --file packages/Webkul/Sales/src/Models/Order.php
#     app caller files : 13    SCATTER (dirs) : 10
#     GREP-NOISE : 'Order' in 131 app files      PRECISION=0.10   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   $ python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Product --propose --file packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php
#     app caller files : 35    SCATTER (dirs) : 17
#     GREP-NOISE : 'Product' in 308 app files    PRECISION=0.11   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   $ python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Address --propose --file packages/Webkul/Core/src/Models/Address.php
#     app caller files : 0     SCATTER (dirs) : 0
#     GREP-NOISE : 'Address' in 98 app files     PRECISION=0.00   colocated + RESOLVER-GAP?
# Product is KEPT (the re-question rule keeps the anchor) and the profile agrees: 0.11 against
# Order's 0.10 is inside noise, Order yields 13 dependent files against Product's 35, and
# Address yields none from that path. 0.11 sits in the band the calibration names (a banked
# +1.00 cell profiled at 0.078). Precision RANKS; it never kills, and no grep census was run
# against any row below.
# SCRIPT NOTE (one line, not fixed, per SCOPE): seam_hunt.py reports 0 app callers and
# PRECISION=0.00 with RESOLVER-GAP? for every symbol when `--file` is omitted; passing
# `--file` is what makes it resolve, and that is the invocation quoted above.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN (step 3), at MCP defaults, no min_confidence passed:
#   $ python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE \
#       '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Product","file":"packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php"}}]'
#   risk high, risk_factors ["93 direct callers"], direct_callers 55 rows over 35 FILES,
#   affected_symbols 310, production_affected 130, affected_via_composition 25,
#   affected_subclasses 25, importers_not_reached 3, retained_via_interfaces_count 31 with
#   retained_trimmed true, truncated true, snippets_truncated true.
#   (A bare `symbol: "Product"` returns ambiguous with 9 matches; the `file` argument above is
#   what the hint tells the arm to pass.)
#   ALL 18 dependents rows below sit inside a shown `direct_callers` row; the enclosing
#   [def start-end] is recorded on each row and was checked against the payload, not assumed.
#
# MEMORIZATION (step 5), and what it changes here:
#   $ python3 bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Product --json /tmp/mem4.json
#   ok:false  reason: "closed-book recall 0.458 (33 members named) >= 0.3 - recited"
#   samples 0.458 / 0.444 / 0.417, said_n 41 of truth_n 72
#   hits include: attribute_family, attribute_values, attributesToArray, bundle_options,
#     categories, channels, checkInLoadedFamilyAttributes, cross_sells, downloadable_links,
#     downloadable_samples, getAttribute, getTypeInstance, grouped_products, images,
#     inventories, inventory_indices, inventory_sources
# What is recited is the RECORD'S OWN MEMBER SURFACE, and every recited member sits in the
# non-scoring `contract` group. No dependent SITE is recitable from it - the probe measures
# the anchor's members, not the files that call them - so no row below is cut on memorization.
# What it does change: `getTypeInstance` is recited, so the ask must not lean on that member
# being obscure, and it does not. The distinction this scenario scores is WHICH resolution a
# site reached, which the member name is silent about.
# ============================================================================

name: Bagisto work session - audit which resolution of the catalogue record's kind each site talks to
repo: bagisto
contract_symbol: Webkul\Product\Models\Product
contract_file: packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer of this store platform, about to rework the record the whole catalogue
  hangs off. Today that record keeps its kind as a plain stored string and, the first time
  anyone asks it what it can do, it reads a class name out of a config map keyed by that
  string, builds an instance and hands itself to it. Everything a record can do is answered by
  that object rather than by the record.
  The rework makes the kind explicit and removes the runtime lookup. The reason that is
  dangerous is not that the object is used in many places. It is that the same lookup, on the
  same stored value, is performed in more than one place independently, so two sites that read
  identically are talking to two different objects built from two different copies of the
  value - and a third kind of site never reaches an object at all, because it took the stored
  string and decided for itself.
  So the question the rework turns on is identity: at each place, which of those resolutions
  is actually being talked to, and what carried the site to it.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: what the record is, where the lookup lives,
  what the map it reads is keyed by, and how the record hands itself to the object it builds;
  and then, separately, every place downstream whose behaviour depends on one of those
  resolutions, with the resolution named and the line pinned.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set of
  names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not count,
  and a missed place is a broken storefront shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the catalogue record contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it. Three things.
      First, the record itself. Name it precisely - the fully qualified name of the class and
      the exact file:line it is declared at - and say what code holding one can do with it,
      grouped by job rather than listed routine by routine, with the exact file:line where
      each named routine is defined. This platform gives the same short name to more than one
      thing, so say which one you settled on and how you told them apart, at file:line each.
      Second, the lookup. Give the exact line where the record turns its stored kind into a
      class and builds an instance, the exact line where it hands ITSELF to that instance, the
      file:line of the map that lookup reads and what that map is keyed by, and the base class
      every such instance extends, at file:line, naming which part of that base's state is the
      record. Say what happens when the stored value matches nothing in the map, at file:line.
      Also name the one layer whose stated job is to create and change these records, at
      file:line, and say in a phrase what it is responsible for.
      Third, and deliberately small: say once, briefly, that the same lookup is written more
      than once in this codebase, and give ONE other line where it appears - not the record's.
      Do not enumerate the rest here; I am not asking for that list yet and it earns nothing.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The record named precisely with its declaring file:line and disambiguated from the same-named siblings, its own surface grouped by job with each routine located at file:line, the line where the stored kind is turned into a class, the line where the record hands itself to the instance, the map's file:line and what it is keyed by, the failure line when the value matches nothing, the base class at file:line with the part of its state that is the record, and the declared write layer at file:line
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the catalogue record contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. Take it as settled that the rework is happening: the kind becomes
      explicit and the runtime lookup goes away.
      Answer the only question that matters after that. For every site in this codebase whose
      behaviour depends on one of these resolutions, say which resolution it is actually
      talking to - and where a site reaches one indirectly, name the method that carried it
      there. Two sites can read almost identically and be talking to different ones; some are
      talking to none, and those are the sites that will fail silently.
      The unit of the answer is a site, not a package and not a file, and one file can hold
      more than one. The record, the map, the base class and the create-and-update layer are
      not answers here: you pinned those in the first part and naming them again earns nothing.
      For each site the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line and the
      routine it sits in; which resolution it reaches; and the method that carried it there,
      pinned at its own file:line, which will usually be in a different file from the site.
      Keep it short - the coverage, the resolution, the carrier line, not the prose.
      Organise by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when every
      site inside it is listed separately with its own location. If you run short, say what you
      had still not looked at rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no
      resolution named is half an answer, and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a
      group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every site located at the exact file:line with the routine it sits in named, the resolution it is actually talking to identified, and the carrying method pinned at its own file:line
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the record, its empty marker interface, the swap-in proxy, the config map,
  # ---- the declared write path and the base of the per-kind family. Step 1's answer,
  # ---- deliberately NON-SCORING. A FREE ROW IS A GIFT TO THE BASELINE, and these are exactly
  # ---- the rows both arms reach in one lookup - and the ones memorization_probe shows this
  # ---- model recites closed-book (recall 0.458 over Product's member list). Putting them
  # ---- here removes the floor they would otherwise put under the plain arm. Every line was
  # ---- opened and read this session. None appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-product
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Models/Product.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php:25 `class Product extends Model implements ProductContract` - the record itself; :347 `public function getTypeInstance(): AbstractType` performs the lookup at :353 `$this->typeInstance = app(config('product_types.'.$this->type.'.class'));`, throws at :356 when the stored value matches nothing in the map, and hands ITSELF outward at :359 `$this->typeInstance->setProduct($this);`; :299/:311/:323/:335/:412 (`isSaleable`, `isStockable`, `totalQuantity`, `haveSufficientQuantity`, `getEditableAttributes`) are the forwarders that let a caller reach the resolution without writing the lookup"
  - id: c:product-types-map
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Config/product_types.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Config/product_types.php - the map every one of the independent lookups reads, keyed by the record's stored kind string and returning a `class` entry per kind; it is the single file the rework replaces and the reason no call edge exists from a site to the class it ends up using"
  - id: c:abstract-type
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Type/AbstractType.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Type/AbstractType.php:32 `abstract class AbstractType` holding :39 `protected $product;` (typed only in the docblock at :37, `@var Product`) - the base every resolved instance extends and the receiving end of the handle pushed at Product.php:359; the one holder whose grip on the record IS written down, pinned in step 1 so step 2 is about the sites that reach it without one"
  - id: c:product-repository
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Repositories/ProductRepository.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Repositories/ProductRepository.php:44 `public function model(): string` returning :46 `return Product::class;`, and creating THROUGH a fourth independent lookup at :56 `$typeInstance = app(config('product_types.'.$data['type'].'.class'));` then :58 `$product = $typeInstance->create($data);` - the declared write layer named in step 1, non-scoring"
  - id: c:product-contract
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Contracts/Product.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Contracts/Product.php:5 `interface Product {}` - an EMPTY marker interface implemented by the model at Product.php:25, carrying no members, so nothing about the contract can be learned by reading it; it exists so the concrete class can be swapped, and it is one of the several things in this repo answering to the same short name"
  - id: c:product-proxy
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Models/ProductProxy.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/ProductProxy.php:7 `class ProductProxy extends ModelProxy {}` - the swap-in indirection every relation declaration writes instead of the model class (`ProductProxy::modelClass()`), which is why a neighbouring row's relation declaration never names the model"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: 18 files across 15 areas, one row per file. Each row is a site whose
  # ---- behaviour depends on a resolution of the stored kind, and each row names WHICH
  # ---- resolution. Three classes, and the class is the scored fact:
  # ----   [R] the RECORD'S own resolution (Product.php:353, handle pushed at :359)
  # ----   [L] a LINE ITEM'S own, independent resolution from its own copy of the string
  # ----       (CartItem.php:50, OrderItem.php:59) - the record may not be loaded at all
  # ----   [N] NO resolution: the site takes the raw stored string, or the static helper
  # ----       resolves it with no record in play (ProductType.php:18)
  # ---- Every row is inside a `direct_callers` row of the SHOWN blast payload (enclosing def
  # ---- in brackets, checked against the payload). Every pinned line was opened and read this
  # ---- session; the credit is what the line does, not a script tally.
  # ---- CUT from previous cycles: d:repo-media-filetype (ProductMediaRepository.php:107
  # ---- branches on an upload-type string, not on the record's kind - it does not belong on
  # ---- this axis), d:repo-bundle-options / d:repo-customizable-options /
  # ---- d:repo-downloadable-links / d:admin-simple-customizable / d:admin-virtual-customizable
  # ---- (per-kind write repositories whose dependency is a bare collection read, the shape
  # ---- cycle 2's disjunctive grep printed wholesale), d:helper-configurable-memo and
  # ---- d:shop-api-related (ordinary declared relation reads on the accessor sweep's own
  # ---- output), d:flat-indexer-refresh (taken by the plain arm 1/1 in cycle 1 AND 1/1 in
  # ---- cycle 2 under two different asks - two independent observations, which is the
  # ---- consistency test; Flat.php is kept below but re-pinned to :124, a different line and
  # ---- a different class).

  # -- area: the concrete per-kind classes. The abstract base is an anchor; these are not.
  - id: d:type-bundle-children
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Type/Bundle.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Type/Bundle.php:150 `return array_unique($this->product->bundle_options()->pluck('product_id')->toArray());` inside `public function getChildrenIds()` at :148 [shown def 148-151] - [R]: this file IS one of the resolved classes; it reads the record out of an inherited field it never assigns, because the handle was pushed in from outside at Product.php:359, so no line here says where `$this->product` came from. MEASURED: missed by the plain arm in cycle 2, taken in cycle 3"
  - id: d:type-configurable-children
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Type/Configurable.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Type/Configurable.php:296 `return $this->product->variants()->pluck('id')->toArray();` inside `public function getChildrenIds()` at :294 [shown def 294-297] - [R]: the same inherited field and the same externally-pushed handle, giving a DIFFERENT answer to the same question; the two sibling files never reference each other and only the lookup at Product.php:353 decides which one a caller reaches. MEASURED: missed by the plain arm in cycle 2, taken in cycle 3"

  # -- area: the per-kind price indexers, which resolve a SECOND time per child row
  - id: d:price-indexer-configurable
    group: dependents
    match: [Indexers/Price/Configurable.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Helpers/Indexers/Price/Configurable.php:35 `if (! $variant->getTypeInstance()->isSaleable()) {` inside `public function getMinimalPrice($qty = null)` at :30 [shown def 30-58] - [R], but on the VARIANT and not on the record the indexer was given: each child row runs its own lookup from its own stored kind, so one call to this routine resolves as many times as the parent has variants, and :39 then asks that per-variant instance for its own price indexer"
  - id: d:price-indexer-bundle
    group: dependents
    match: [Indexers/Price/Bundle.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Helpers/Indexers/Price/Bundle.php:116 `if (! $variant->getTypeInstance()->isSaleable()) {` inside `public function getMaximumPrice()` at :108 [shown def 108-152] - [R] on the option's associated record, reached two hops out through `$option->bundle_option_products` then `$bundleOptionProduct->product` at :114; :120 chains a second resolution to fetch that instance's own price indexer and pushes the variant back into it with `setProduct($variant)`"

  # -- area: the flat indexer, which resolves through the STATIC helper with no record in play
  - id: d:flat-indexer-hasvariants
    group: dependents
    match: [Indexers/Flat.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Helpers/Indexers/Flat.php:124 `if (ProductType::hasVariants($product->type)) {` inside `public function refresh($product)` at :118 [shown def 118-133] - [N]: the record is present but is NOT asked; its raw stored string is handed to a static helper which runs its own lookup at ProductType.php:18 `$type = app(config('product_types.'.$typeKey.'.class'));` and throws the instance away. The record's own resolution is never touched, so nothing links this site to the record's instance"

  # -- area: the admin request/resource layer, reached through a wrapper that forwards
  - id: d:admin-product-form-rules
    group: dependents
    match: [Admin/src/Http/Requests/ProductForm.php]
